{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>For, etc.</b> Some say that the meaning is as if, and Rabbi Judah the Grammarian, may his memory be for a blessing, said that the meaning of the creation of heavens and earth concerns particulars. The correct view is that the heavens are the firmament, and HaShem will renew the air so that human beings will be healthy in body and live many years; and He will also increase the power of the earth, and behold it is new. And one who says that this is an allusion to the World to Come does not adhere to the passage, for there is no eating in the World to Come, and so our early ones, of blessed memory, indicated [Berakhot 17], and He alone is truth:" "<b>The former things.</b> Some say: concerning the troubles, and this is the correct view. Others said: concerning the heavens and the earth, and there is no taste in this interpretation. The proof is the verse that follows it:"], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 65, :notes {0 "Rabbi Judah the Grammarian identified here is Judah ibn Tibbon; the epithet נ\"ע (may his soul be bound [in the bond of life]) rendered 'may his memory be for a blessing' following the conventional English equivalent for this honorific applied to the recently deceased."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:01:07.389707728Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}